## 10. QUICK CAPTURE AND PRODUCTIVITY SHORTCUTS

Fast task entry is essential.

Implement:

- Global quick-add interface.
- Keyboard shortcut to open quick-add.
- Enter-to-save behavior where appropriate.
- Quick priority selection.
- Quick intensity selection.
- Optional date and project assignment.
- Natural, readable date shortcuts using conventional parsing.
- Keyboard navigation.
- Contextual task menus.
- Searchable command palette.

All parsing must be deterministic.

For example, if a user enters a task followed by an unambiguous supported date, the application may interpret the date and show the result before saving.

Never silently interpret ambiguous input as a deadline.

Always allow the user to correct the parsed result.

## 11. FOCUS MODE

Implement an optional focus mode.

Features:

- Select a task.
- Start a focus session.
- Choose a duration or use an open-ended timer.
- Pause and resume.
- Finish or cancel the session.
- Record completed focus sessions.
- Display time spent on a task.
- Show session history.

Do not force users to follow the Pomodoro technique.

Support longer sessions for people who prefer uninterrupted work.

Keep the interface minimal while the timer is running.

Do not add distracting gamification.

## 12. INSIGHTS AND PROGRESS

Create a visually refined Insights page.

Show meaningful information rather than vanity metrics.

Include:

- Tasks completed over time.
- Completion trends.
- Habit consistency.
- Weekly and monthly summaries.
- Focus time.
- Distribution of task intensity.
- Overdue-task trends.
- Personal progress history.

Make time ranges selectable.

Use accessible charts with clear labels, tooltips, and empty states.

Do not fabricate statistics.

Calculate metrics from actual stored data and define each metric consistently.

Clearly distinguish estimated workload from measured time spent.

The purpose is to help users understand their routines and make better decisions, not to judge their productivity.

## 13. DAILY ENGAGEMENT WITHOUT MANIPULATION

Create an experience that people naturally want to return to.

Use:

- Satisfying completion feedback.
- Visible progress.
- Subtle transitions.
- Personalized themes.
- Useful widgets where the platform supports them.
- Optional daily planning reminders.
- Optional evening review reminders.
- Weekly summaries.
- A sense of continuity through historical progress.

Avoid:

- Punitive streak resets.
- Shame-based messages.
- Artificial scarcity.
- Excessive notifications.
- Endless reward animations.
- Mandatory daily check-ins.
- Fake social pressure.
- Notifications designed solely to increase screen time.

A user should feel better after using the application, not anxious about keeping it alive.

## 14. CROSS-PLATFORM SYNCHRONIZATION

Cross-device synchronization is a core requirement.

A task created on one device must eventually appear on the user's other signed-in devices.

A completion recorded on mobile must propagate to desktop.

A habit logged offline must be preserved and synchronized when connectivity returns.

Implement:

- Email-based authentication.
- Secure session handling.
- Optional supported OAuth sign-in.
- Per-user data isolation.
- Local persistence.
- Offline task creation and editing.
- A queued synchronization mechanism.
- Background synchronization when supported.
- Retry handling.
- Duplicate prevention.
- Conflict resolution.
- Clear synchronization status.
- Loading and error states.
- Account sign-out.
- Account data export.
- Account deletion.

Use stable identifiers and appropriate timestamps.

Do not treat the last device to connect as automatically correct in every conflict.

Define explicit conflict-resolution rules for simultaneous edits.

Never silently discard unsynchronized changes.

Clearly communicate when data is waiting to sync.

Use secure database access policies so users cannot read or modify other users' private data.

Never expose server secrets in client-side code.

### Offline behavior

The core experience must remain useful without an internet connection.

Users must be able to:

- View previously loaded tasks and habits.
- Create tasks.
- Edit tasks.
- Complete tasks.
- Record habit progress.
- Review locally available history.

Queue supported changes and synchronize them later.

Document platform limitations for background synchronization and notifications.

Do not claim synchronization works until it has been tested on multiple browser sessions or devices.

